@meta
id: 25-11
title: Every line, one normal command
chapter: 25
type: lesson
skills: ex-normal
par: 19
minutes: 5
vscode: Alt-click the end of every line, type the semicolon once
open: analytics.sql

@brief
Eight queries pasted out of the dashboard, none of them terminated. Every
line needs a semicolon on the end. There are no blank lines and no
exceptions.

You could do this with a macro, or with `:%s/$/;/`. Reach instead for the
command this chapter is named after. `:normal` takes a string of keys and
presses them, in normal mode, once for every line in its range. `A;` is
what you would press on one line. `:%normal A;` is what you press for all
of them.

That is the whole idea: any normal-mode edit you can do to one line, you
can do to a range of lines by putting `:%normal` in front of it.

@keys
| `:%normal A;` | press `A;` on every line in the file |
| `:5,20normal x` | the same keys, on lines 5 to 20 |
| `:normal` | on the current line only |

@hints
Work out the keys for one line first. Appending to the end of a line is
two keys.
--
Then put the range and the command in front of them, and do not press
Esc — the range ends the insert for you.
--
`:%normal A;` and Enter, then `:wq`.

@solution
    :%normal A;<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Sixteen keystrokes for eight lines, and the same sixteen for eight
hundred.

Two things vim does for you here, both worth knowing:

**Insert mode is closed at the end of every line.** `A;` leaves you in
insert mode when you type it by hand; `:normal` behaves as if an Esc were
pressed after the last key, per line. So `A;` is complete as written and
`A;<Esc>` would be wrong — the Esc has to be spelled differently, which
is the subject of a later lesson.

**The range is worked out before the command runs.** `:%normal dd` does
not delete every other line the way a naive loop would; vim tracks the
lines as they shift.

Use `:normal!` in anything you save — a script, a mapping, a vimrc. The
bang ignores the player's own mappings, so the keys mean what the manual
says they mean rather than what someone's config made them mean. Typed
by hand on your own machine, plain `:normal` is fine.

The obvious question is how this differs from `:g//normal` from chapter
15. `:g` picks the lines by pattern and then acts; `:normal` acts on
every line of a range you have described. They compose:
`:/BEGIN/,/END/g/status/normal A,` is a perfectly ordinary command.

@start analytics.sql
SELECT count(*) FROM orders WHERE status = 'PENDING'
SELECT count(*) FROM orders WHERE status = 'SHIPPED'
SELECT count(*) FROM orders WHERE status = 'CANCELLED'
SELECT sum(total) FROM orders WHERE created_at > now() - interval '1 day'
SELECT customer_id, count(*) FROM orders GROUP BY customer_id
SELECT count(*) FROM refunds WHERE settled_at IS NULL
SELECT avg(total) FROM orders WHERE status = 'SHIPPED'
SELECT count(*) FROM payments WHERE gateway = 'legacy'

@expected analytics.sql
SELECT count(*) FROM orders WHERE status = 'PENDING';
SELECT count(*) FROM orders WHERE status = 'SHIPPED';
SELECT count(*) FROM orders WHERE status = 'CANCELLED';
SELECT sum(total) FROM orders WHERE created_at > now() - interval '1 day';
SELECT customer_id, count(*) FROM orders GROUP BY customer_id;
SELECT count(*) FROM refunds WHERE settled_at IS NULL;
SELECT avg(total) FROM orders WHERE status = 'SHIPPED';
SELECT count(*) FROM payments WHERE gateway = 'legacy';
